Here is a summary of the changes for each section of the bill, starting with Sec. 10101.

**Sec. 10101. Re-evaluation of thrifty food plan.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to redefine the "thrifty food plan" as the diet for a family of four (a man and woman ages 20-50, and children ages 6-8 and 9-11) based on the 2021 report. It sets household adjustment ratios (e.g., 30% for a 1-person household, 100% for a 4-person household) and requires cost adjustments for Hawaii and Alaska. The Secretary may re-evaluate the market baskets not earlier than October 1, 2027, but cannot increase the plan's cost based on that re-evaluation.

**Sec. 10102. Modifications to SNAP work requirements for able-bodied adults.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to expand exceptions to SNAP work requirements for individuals under 18 or over 65, the medically unfit, parents of children under 14, pregnant women, and certain Indians. It standardizes enforcement by defining a "noncontiguous State" and allows the Secretary to exempt such states from work requirements if they demonstrate a good faith effort, with exemptions expiring by December 31, 2028.

**Sec. 10103. Availability of standard utility allowances based on receipt of energy assistance.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to restrict standard utility allowances to households with an elderly or disabled member. It also modifies rules for third-party energy assistance payments, distinguishing between households with and without an elderly or disabled member.

**Sec. 10104. Restrictions on internet expenses.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to prohibit any service fee associated with an internet connection from being used in computing the excess shelter expense deduction for SNAP benefits.

**Sec. 10105. Matching funds requirements.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to establish a state quality control incentive based on payment error rates beginning in fiscal year 2028. It sets federal and state cost-share percentages (e.g., 100% federal/0% state for error rates under 6%, down to 85% federal/15% state for rates of 10% or more) and allows states to elect which error rate to use for fiscal year 2028. It also delays implementation for states with high error rates and limits the Secretary's authority to pay above the applicable federal share.

**Sec. 10106. Administrative cost sharing.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to change the federal share of administrative costs for state agencies from 50 percent to 25 percent for fiscal year 2027 and each fiscal year thereafter.

**Sec. 10107. National education and obesity prevention grant program.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to extend mandatory funding for the program through fiscal year 2025, rather than for fiscal year 2016 and each subsequent fiscal year.

**Sec. 10108. Alien SNAP eligibility.** Amends the Food and Nutrition Act of 2008 to restrict SNAP eligibility to individuals who are residents of the United States and are either citizens, nationals, lawful permanent residents, Cuban/Haitian entrants, or individuals lawfully residing under a Compact of Free Association. It requires the income and resources of an ineligible individual to be considered in determining the eligibility and allotment of their household.

**Sec. 10201. Rescission of amounts for forestry.** Rescinds unobligated balances appropriated by specified paragraphs of sections 23001, 23002, 23003, and 23005 of Public Law 117-169 (the Inflation Reduction Act).

**Sec. 10301. Effective reference price; reference price.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to set new reference prices for covered commodities (e.g., wheat at $6.35/bushel, corn at $4.10/bushel) beginning with the 2025 crop year. It increases the effective reference price multiplier from 85% to 88% and provides for annual adjustments beginning in the 2031 crop year, capped at 113 percent of the initial reference price.

**Sec. 10302. Base acres.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to extend the base acre calculation period to 2031 and authorizes the Secretary to allocate up to 30,000,000 additional base acres to eligible farms beginning with the 2026 crop year. Eligibility is based on a farm's 5-year average plantings of covered and noncovered commodities (2019-2023) exceeding its current base acres, with a pro-rata reduction if total eligible acres exceed 30,000,000. It also establishes payment yields for new base acres and provides rules for new owners.

**Sec. 10303. Producer election.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to extend the producer election period for crop coverage through 2031 and adds a 2026 crop year election. It requires the same coverage for the 2027 through 2031 crop years as was applicable for the 2025 crop year and mandates that for the 2025 crop year, the Secretary make the higher of price loss coverage or agriculture risk coverage payments on a commodity-by-commodity basis. It also amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to remove a reference to producers electing agriculture risk coverage.

**Sec. 10304. Price loss coverage.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to extend price loss coverage through 2031 and update payment yield provisions. It changes the calculation for payment yields to use 2017 through 2021 crop years and updates cross-references to the reference price section.

**Sec. 10305. Agriculture risk coverage.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to extend agriculture risk coverage through 2031 and modify the benchmark revenue calculation. It sets the benchmark revenue at 90 percent of the benchmark revenue for the 2025 through 2031 crop years and increases the maximum payment rate from 10 percent to 12 percent of benchmark revenue for those years.

**Sec. 10306. Equitable treatment of certain entities.** Amends the Food Security Act of 1985 to replace the term "joint venture or general partnership" with "qualified pass-through entity" (defined as a partnership, S corporation, limited liability company, or joint venture) for payment eligibility purposes. It also makes conforming amendments to provisions on attribution of payments, persons actively engaged in farming, joint and several liability, and exclusions from adjusted gross income calculations.

**Sec. 10307. Payment limitations.** Amends the Food Security Act of 1985 to increase the per-person and per-entity payment limitation from $125,000 to $155,000, subject to an inflation adjustment. It requires the Secretary to annually adjust these amounts for inflation based on the Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers for the 2025 crop year and each crop year thereafter.

**Sec. 10308. Adjusted gross income limitation.** Amends the Food Security Act of 1985 to create an exception to the adjusted gross income limitation for certain operations. The limitation does not apply to a person or legal entity if at least 75 percent of their average gross income derives from farming, ranching, or silviculture activities, including agri-tourism and direct-to-consumer marketing, for excepted payments or benefits.

**Sec. 10309. Marketing loans.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to extend nonrecourse marketing assistance loans through 2031 and set new loan rates for the 2026 through 2031 crop years (e.g., wheat at $3.72/bushel, corn at $2.42/bushel). It also extends provisions for loan deficiency payments, payments in lieu of LDPs, special competitive provisions for extra long staple cotton, and recourse loans through 2031, and increases the cotton storage payment rate.

**Sec. 10310. Repayment of marketing loans.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to modify repayment rates for marketing assistance loans, including for long grain rice, medium grain rice, and upland cotton. It provides a refund for upland cotton when repaid at a rate based on the world market price and sets a repayment rate that is the lesser of the loan rate or the prevailing world market price. It also extends special provisions for extra long staple cotton through July 31, 2032.

**Sec. 10311. Economic adjustment assistance for textile mills.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to increase the value of assistance for textile mills from 3 cents per pound to 5 cents per pound beginning on August 1, 2025.

**Sec. 10312. Sugar program updates.** Amends the Federal Agriculture Improvement and Reform Act of 1996 and the Agricultural Adjustment Act of 1938 to update the sugar program. It sets the raw cane sugar loan rate at 24.00 cents per pound for the 2025 through 2031 crop years, establishes a refined sugar loan rate at 136.55 percent of the raw cane rate, and updates storage rates. It also extends sugar estimates through 2031, modifies beet sugar allotments, and requires a study on refined sugar imports.

**Sec. 10313. Dairy policy updates.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to modify the Dairy Margin Coverage program. It changes the production history calculation to the highest annual milk marketings during any of the 2021, 2022, or 2023 calendar years and increases the Tier I and Tier II production threshold from 5,000,000 pounds to 6,000,000 pounds. It also extends the program through 2031 and updates premium discount provisions.

**Sec. 10314. Implementation.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to provide $50,000,000 in further funding to carry out the subtitle, with specific allocations for program implementation, surveys, and a study on refined sugar imports.

**Sec. 10401. Supplemental agricultural disaster assistance.** Amends the Agricultural Act of 2014 to modify disaster assistance programs. It sets livestock indemnity payment rates at 100 percent of market value for predation losses and 75 percent for adverse weather or disease losses, and provides additional payments for unborn livestock death losses. It also modifies the livestock forage disaster program, provides emergency assistance for farm-raised fish losses due to bird depredation, sets a 15 percent normal mortality rate for honeybee losses, and modifies the Tree Assistance Program.

**Sec. 10501. Beginning farmer and rancher benefit.** Amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to increase the duration of the beginning farmer and rancher benefit from 5 years to 10 years. It also provides additional premium assistance of 5 percentage points for the first two reinsurance years, 3 percentage points for the third year, and 1 percentage point for the fourth year.

**Sec. 10502. Area-based crop insurance coverage and affordability.** Amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to increase the maximum coverage level for area-based crop insurance to 95 percent and for individual coverage aggregated across multiple commodities to 90 percent. It also increases the premium subsidy for area-based coverage from 65 percent to 80 percent.

**Sec. 10503. Administrative and operating expense adjustments.** Amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to provide additional administrative and operating expense subsidies to approved insurance providers. It establishes a 6 percent payment for eligible contracts in eligible states (where the loss ratio exceeds 120 percent of net book premium), sets a minimum reimbursement rate for specialty crops, and requires an inflation adjustment for total A&O expenses beginning with the 2026 reinsurance year.

**Sec. 10504. Premium support.** Amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to increase premium subsidy percentages for various coverage levels. For example, the subsidy for 75 percent coverage increases from 64 percent to 69 percent, and for 50 percent coverage from 38 percent to 41 percent.

**Sec. 10505. Program compliance and integrity.** Amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to increase mandatory funding for compliance and integrity activities from $4,000,000 for each of fiscal years 2009 through 2025 to $6,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each subsequent fiscal year.

**Sec. 10506. Reviews, compliance, and integrity.** Amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to increase mandatory funding for reviews, compliance, and integrity from an unspecified amount for each fiscal year to $10,000,000 for fiscal year 2026 and each fiscal year thereafter.

**Sec. 10507. Poultry insurance pilot program.** Amends the Federal Crop Insurance Act to require the Corporation to establish a pilot program for contract poultry growers to receive index-based insurance for extreme weather-related risk. The program must be conducted in a sufficient number of counties in top poultry-producing states, and the Board must approve a policy or plan based on the pilot within 2 years.

**Sec. 10601. Conservation.** Amends the Food Security Act of 1985 to provide mandatory funding for conservation programs for fiscal years 2026 through 2031. It sets specific funding levels for the Conservation Reserve Program, the Regional Conservation Partnership Program, the Grassroots Source Water Protection Program, the Voluntary Public Access and Habitat Incentive Program, and the Watershed Protection and Flood Prevention program. It also rescinds unobligated balances from the Inflation Reduction Act.

**Sec. 10602. Supplemental agricultural trade promotion program.** Requires the Secretary of Agriculture to carry out a program to encourage the accessibility, development, maintenance, and expansion of commercial export markets for U.S. agricultural commodities. It provides $285,000,000 from the Commodity Credit Corporation for fiscal year 2027 and each fiscal year thereafter.

**Sec. 10603. Nutrition.** Amends the Emergency Food Assistance Act of 1983 to extend the authorization of appropriations for the program through 2031.

**Sec. 10604. Research.** Amends various research and extension provisions to provide mandatory funding. It extends funding for the urban agriculture research initiative through 2031, provides $37,000,000 to the Foundation for Food and Agriculture Research, $60,000,000 for scholarships at 1890 institutions, $8,000,000 for the assistive technology program, and $175,000,000 for the specialty crop research initiative for fiscal year 2026. It also provides $125,000,000 for research facilities for fiscal year 2026 and each fiscal year thereafter.

**Sec. 10605. Energy.** Amends the Farm Security and Rural Investment Act of 2002 to extend the authorization of appropriations for the energy program through 2031.

**Sec. 10606. Horticulture.** Amends the Plant Protection Act and other laws to provide mandatory funding for horticulture programs. It provides $75,000,000 for plant pest and disease management for each of fiscal years 2018 through 2025 and $90,000,000 for fiscal year 2026, and increases specialty crop block grant funding to $100,000,000 for fiscal year 2026. It also provides $10,000,000 for the organic production and market data initiative for fiscal years 2026 through 2031 and $5,000,000 for a crop and pesticide use survey for fiscal year 2026.

**Sec. 10607. Miscellaneous.** Amends various laws to provide mandatory funding for miscellaneous programs. It provides $233,000,000 for animal disease prevention for each of fiscal years 2026 through 2030 and $75,000,000 for fiscal year 2031 and each fiscal year thereafter. It also provides $3,000,000 for the sheep production and marketing grant program for fiscal year 2026 and extends the authorization for several trust funds and programs through 2031. [end of text]


